Content: JobExec   Starting from 2.0.1,  JobExec  serves as a replacement to  JCLExec , which is deprecated. 
 This command is used to submit a JCL job and retrieve the status and job outputs. The target JCL source can be located in a sequential data set, a PDS or PDSE member, a file on zFS, or dynamically created in the build script and passed in as a           Java String. 
 JobExec  serves as a replacement to  JCLExec  and does not rely on the System Display and Search Facility (SDSF). Like  JCLExec ,  JobExec  submits jobs by using the default  life-of-job SSIB . 
 Note:  Because  JobExec  interacts directly with the JESx spool, job output has to remain on the spool long enough to gather status and output. Therefore, a job class that does not immediately archive or delete the output           must be used. 
 Example 1:  Execute JCL from a data set. 
 JobExec.execute()  returns the  max rc  of the job, unlike  JCLExec  which only returned 0 or 1. Therefore, it is now preferable to use the integer  rc  code returned by  execute()  rather           than the string returned by  getMaxRC() . 
 def  rc =  new  JobExec().dataset( "USR1.JCL" ).member( "TEST" ).execute()
   Example 2:  Execute JCL from a file on HFS. 
 def  rc =  new  JobExec().file( new  File( "/u/usr1/jcl/test.jcl" )).execute()
   Example 3:  Execute JCL from a String value in the script. 
 def  jcl =  '''\
//HELLO  JOB ,
//       MSGCLASS=H,MSGLEVEL=(1,1),TIME=(,4),REGION=0M
//*
//* PRINT \"HELLO WORLD\" ON JOB OUTPUT
//*
//STEP0001 EXEC PGM=IEBGENER
//SYSIN    DD DUMMY
//SYSPRINT DD SYSOUT=*
//SYSUT1   DD *
HELLO, WORLD
/*
//SYSUT2   DD SYSOUT=*
//
''' 

 def  rc =  new  JobExec().text(jcl).execute()
   Example 4:  Execute JCL and get output information. 
 def  jobExec =  new  JobExec().dataset( "USR1.JCL" ).member( "TEST" )
 def  rc = jobExec.execute()

 def  jobID = jobExec.getSubmittedJobId()
 def  jobName = jobExec.getSubmittedJobName()
 def  listDDNames = jobExec.getAllDDNames( true )
JobExec
